1
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
You just cobble something together
to sell. It need not be any good.

2
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:38,000
As long as you can fool people
into buying it...

3
00:00:38,001 --> 00:00:43,000
...you can always try to make
better versions later.

4
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:51,000
So then you get these version numbers...

5
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:57,000
even with decimals, version 2.6 or 2.7,
That nonsense.

6
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:07,000
While version 1 should have been
the finished product.

7
00:01:10,001 --> 00:01:15,000
Computing science
is no more about computers...

8
00:01:15,001 --> 00:01:19,000
...than astronomy is about telescopes.

9
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:29,000
Professor Edsger Wybe Dijkstra
is Holland's first programmer

10
00:01:30,001 --> 00:01:35,000
In 1972 he received the Turing Award...

11
00:01:36,001 --> 00:01:38,000
...the Nobel Prize of computing science.

12
00:01:39,001 --> 00:01:45,000
He and his wife now live in Austin,
Texas, where they moved in 1984.

13
00:01:45,001 --> 00:01:52,000
At the time, most university departments
in the Netherlands...

14
00:01:52,001 --> 00:01:55,000
..aimed to water down their curriculum

15
00:01:57,001 --> 00:02:04,000
At the same time
the university of Texas in Austin...

16
00:02:05,001 --> 00:02:11,000
...tried to reduce the student enrollment...

17
00:02:12,001 --> 00:02:15,000
...and to increase their quality.

18
00:02:15,001 --> 00:02:22,000
It was an opposite development
which was much more attractive...

19
00:02:23,001 --> 00:02:26,000
...that what was happening
in Dutch higher education

20
00:02:40,001 --> 00:02:45,000
The universities lack the courage
to teach hard science.

21
00:02:46,001 --> 00:02:49,000
They will continue to misguide
the students...

22
00:02:49,001 --> 00:02:54,000
...and each stage in the infantilization
of the curriculum will be hailed...

23
00:02:55,001 --> 00:02:57,000
...as educational progress.

24
00:03:00,001 --> 00:03:03,000
Quality, correctness and elegance...

25
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:07,000
...are what Dijkstra thinks should
characterize a computer program.

26
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:13,000
In 1954 he resolved
to make programming a science.

27
00:03:13,001 --> 00:03:17,000
But it has been an uphill struggle.

28
00:03:17,001 --> 00:03:19,000
I lose no sleep...

29
00:03:19,001 --> 00:03:25,000
...that businesses feel
they cannot afford...

30
00:03:25,001 --> 00:03:28,000
....to deliver first-rate products.

31
00:03:30,001 --> 00:03:35,000
It doesn't keep me
from continuing my work.

32
00:03:41,001 --> 00:03:47,000
There are very different
programming styles.

33
00:04:18,001 --> 00:04:20,000
There are very different
programming styles.

34
00:04:20,001 --> 00:04:25,000
I tend to see them
as Mozart versus Beethoven.

35
00:04:25,001 --> 00:04:31,000
When Mozart started to write,
the composition was finished.

36
00:04:32,001 --> 00:04:37,000
He wrote the manuscript in one go.

37
00:04:38,001 --> 00:04:41,000
In beautiful handwriting, too.

38
00:04:42,001 --> 00:04:45,000
Beethoven was a doubter
and a struggler...

39
00:04:45,001 --> 00:04:49,000
...who started writing before he finished
the composition...

40
00:04:49,001 --> 00:04:53,000
...and then glued corrections
onto the page.

41
00:04:53,001 --> 00:04:57,000
In one place he did this nine times.

42
00:04:57,001 --> 00:05:04,000
When they peeled them, the last version
proved identical to the first one.

43
00:05:05,001 --> 00:05:07,000
That iterative method of programming...

44
00:05:08,001 --> 00:05:15,000
...is somehow a very Anglo-Saxon
custom.

45
00:05:15,001 --> 00:05:18,000
British education is pervaded by it.

46
00:05:19,001 --> 00:05:24,000
People learn, when they write,
not to try to get it right the first time.

47
00:05:25,001 --> 00:05:30,000
Just write what's on your mind
and then rewrite repeatedly...

48
00:05:31,001 --> 00:05:34,000
...to get the product you want.

49
00:05:36,001 --> 00:05:42,000
That's partly why word processors
are marketed so aggressively...

50
00:05:42,001 --> 00:05:46,000
...and partly why they have been
so successful there.

51
00:05:46,001 --> 00:05:52,000
While it is one of the advantages
of working with pen and paper...

52
00:05:52,001 --> 00:05:56,000
...that when you start a sentence,
you should have it ready.

53
00:06:11,001 --> 00:06:18,000
Dijkstra is a prolific writer: musings,
talks, mathematical proofs and books.

54
00:06:18,001 --> 00:06:23,000
His EWD's (his computing science papers),
his initials followed by a number.
(EWD = Edsger Wybe Dijkstra)

55
00:06:23,001 --> 00:06:27,000
...are considered
his best scientific contributions.

56
00:06:28,001 --> 00:06:33,000
For myself, the most important thing
has been...

57
00:06:33,001 --> 00:06:39,000
...the daily discipline...

58
00:06:39,001 --> 00:06:43,000
...of neatly writing down your thoughts
and what you do.

59
00:06:49,001 --> 00:06:54,000
Due to modern technology,
they have been much more influential...

60
00:06:55,001 --> 00:06:57,000
...than they would have been in the past.

61
00:06:57,001 --> 00:07:01,000
You might describe them...

62
00:07:03,001 --> 00:07:07,000
...as a modern form
of scientific correspondence.

63
00:07:07,001 --> 00:07:12,000
Albeit that the intellectual traffic
has been mostly one-directional.

64
00:07:13,001 --> 00:07:18,000
The serial numbers have crept up
to over 1300.

65
00:07:18,001 --> 00:07:25,000
In length they vary enormously,
from 80 pages to one page.

66
00:07:25,001 --> 00:07:30,000
As time goes by, they tend to become
ever shorter.

67
00:07:31,001 --> 00:07:35,000
Everyone to whom I sent them...

68
00:07:35,001 --> 00:07:40,000
...was implicitly willing to function...

69
00:07:40,001 --> 00:07:44,000
...as an internal node
of the dissemination tree...

70
00:07:46,001 --> 00:07:52,000
...and to send second generation copies
on to others.

71
00:07:58,001 --> 00:08:05,000
How many people they have reached,
I have never been able to estimate.

72
00:08:05,001 --> 00:08:07,000
A few hundred, I'd say.

73
00:08:24,001 --> 00:08:31,000
In order to compose,
you have to write scores.

74
00:08:31,001 --> 00:08:35,000
To be a composer is to conceive music.

75
00:08:36,001 --> 00:08:43,000
In the early days of programming
you had to write machine code.

76
00:08:45,001 --> 00:08:51,000
Meaningless sequences
of capitals and numbers.

77
00:08:52,001 --> 00:08:56,000
That's the analogue of writing scores.

78
00:08:57,001 --> 00:09:02,000
People thought that
THAT was programming.

79
00:09:04,001 --> 00:09:08,000
Later that was made easier...

80
00:09:09,001 --> 00:09:13,000
...by the invention of the higher
programming languages:

81
00:09:13,001 --> 00:09:17,000
Fortran, Pascal, C++, and such.

82
00:09:20,001 --> 00:09:26,000
People thought
that those languages would...

83
00:09:28,001 --> 00:09:33,000
...solve the programming problem.

84
00:09:34,001 --> 00:09:36,000
But when you looked closely...

85
00:09:36,001 --> 00:09:43,000
...the trivial aspects of programming
had been automated...

86
00:09:43,001 --> 00:09:45,000
...while the hard ones remained.

87
00:09:46,001 --> 00:09:49,000
The higher programming languages...

88
00:09:49,001 --> 00:09:54,000
...which had been intended
to facilitate programming...

89
00:09:54,001 --> 00:10:00,000
...proved, coupled with the increasing
ambitions of the applications...

90
00:10:00,001 --> 00:10:04,000
...to be more intellectually demanding
to the programmer.

91
00:10:07,001 --> 00:10:15,000
EWD340: "The competent programmer is fully aware
of the limited size of his own skull.

92
00:10:15,001 --> 00:10:20,000
He therefore approaches his task
in full humility...

93
00:10:20,001 --> 00:10:24,000
...and avoids clever tricks like the plague."

94
00:10:26,001 --> 00:10:29,000
I remember, in 1970 or thereabouts...

95
00:10:29,001 --> 00:10:37,000
...I first went to explain to companies
how to develop programs...

96
00:10:37,001 --> 00:10:39,000
...and keep them under tight control.

97
00:10:39,001 --> 00:10:42,000
I first went to Paris
and then Brussels.

98
00:10:43,001 --> 00:10:49,000
In Paris I delivered a lecture
at the Sorbonne...

99
00:10:49,001 --> 00:10:52,000
...and people were very enthusiastic.

100
00:10:52,001 --> 00:10:57,000
On the way home I told the same story...

101
00:10:57,001 --> 00:11:01,000
...at a large software house in Brussels.

102
00:11:03,001 --> 00:11:06,000
The lecture was a complete failure.

103
00:11:07,001 --> 00:11:11,000
In a sense, one of my
least successful lectures.

104
00:11:11,001 --> 00:11:14,000
I later found out why.

105
00:11:15,001 --> 00:11:19,000
The management didn't want
faultless programs...

106
00:11:19,001 --> 00:11:25,000
...because the company derived its
stability from maintenance contracts.

107
00:11:26,001 --> 00:11:29,000
And the programmers
weren't interested...

108
00:11:29,001 --> 00:11:34,000
...because they derived
their intellectual excitement...

109
00:11:35,001 --> 00:11:40,000
... from the fact that they didn't quite
know what they were doing.

110
00:11:41,001 --> 00:11:46,000
They felt that if you knew precisely
what you were doing...

111
00:11:47,001 --> 00:11:50,000
...and didn't run risks,
it was a boring job.

112
00:11:52,001 --> 00:11:56,000
We should not introduce errors
through sloppiness...

113
00:11:56,001 --> 00:12:00,000
...but systematically keep them out.

114
00:12:29,001 --> 00:12:33,000
If in physics there's something
you don't understand...

115
00:12:33,001 --> 00:12:41,000
...you can always hide behind
the uncharted depths of nature.

116
00:12:42,001 --> 00:12:47,000
You can always blame God.
You didn't make it so complex yourself.

117
00:12:48,001 --> 00:12:53,000
But if your program doesn't work,
there is no one to hide behind.

118
00:12:55,001 --> 00:12:59,000
You can not hide behind
an obstinate nature.

119
00:12:59,001 --> 00:13:02,000
A zero is a zero, a 1 is a 1.

120
00:13:03,001 --> 00:13:05,000
If it doesn't work, you've messed up.

121
00:13:07,001 --> 00:13:13,000
I realized my previous projects
had just been finger exercises.

122
00:13:13,001 --> 00:13:15,000
I had to tackle complexity itself.

123
00:13:15,001 --> 00:13:19,000
But it took a long time
to muster the courage to do so.

124
00:13:20,001 --> 00:13:25,000
At the end of the 60's,
Dijkstra saw that the complexity...

125
00:13:25,001 --> 00:13:27,000
...got the better of the programmers.

126
00:13:28,001 --> 00:13:32,000
And that it threatened
the most prestigious projects.

127
00:13:32,001 --> 00:13:36,000
That happened in 1969...

128
00:13:36,001 --> 00:13:42,000
...just after the first successful
moon landing.

129
00:13:43,001 --> 00:13:48,000
I was at a NATO conference
on software engineering in Rome...

130
00:13:48,001 --> 00:13:57,000
...where I met Joel Aron, who was head
of IBM's Federal Systems Division...

131
00:13:58,001 --> 00:14:08,000
...which had been responsible
for the software of the moonshot.

132
00:14:09,001 --> 00:14:16,000
I knew that each Apollo flight required
some 40,000 new lines of code.

133
00:14:16,001 --> 00:14:20,000
I don't know what unit
a line of code is...

134
00:14:20,001 --> 00:14:23,000
...but 40,000 is allot.

135
00:14:24,001 --> 00:14:30,000
I was duly impressed that they got
so many lines of code correct.

136
00:14:30,001 --> 00:14:34,000
So when I met Joel, I said:
how did you do that?

137
00:14:35,001 --> 00:14:36,000
Do that? he asked.

138
00:14:37,001 --> 00:14:40,000
Getting that software right.

139
00:14:41,001 --> 00:14:42,000
Right? he said.

140
00:14:43,001 --> 00:14:53,000
He said that in one of the calculations
of the orbit of the lunar module...

141
00:14:55,001 --> 00:14:59,000
..the moon had been defined
as repelling instead of attractive.

142
00:15:00,001 --> 00:15:04,000
They had discovered that error
by accident...

143
00:15:05,001 --> 00:15:07,000
Imagine, BY ACCIDENT...

144
00:15:07,001 --> 00:15:09,000
...five days before the shot.

145
00:15:10,001 --> 00:15:15,000
I went white and said:

146
00:15:15,001 --> 00:15:17,000
Those guys have been lucky!

147
00:15:19,001 --> 00:15:21,000
Yes, Joel Aron agreed.

148
00:15:24,001 --> 00:15:29,000
EWD340: "Program testing can convincingly
show the presence of bugs...

149
00:15:29,001 --> 00:15:35,000
..but it is hopelessly inadequate
to show their absence."

150
00:15:37,001 --> 00:15:43,000
Dijkstra knew personally, how
frighteningly complex programs
could become

151
00:15:43,001 --> 00:15:48,000
He had finished the operating system
of Holland's biggest computer, the X-8...

152
00:15:49,001 --> 00:15:50,000
...under great pressure.

153
00:15:50,001 --> 00:15:55,000
I feared that I wouldn't get it right.
That I would lose control of it.

154
00:15:57,001 --> 00:16:02,000
For the X-8 in Eindhoven...

155
00:16:03,001 --> 00:16:06,000
...we tried to design
a multiprogramming system.

156
00:16:06,001 --> 00:16:13,000
We were working with a machine
with a real-time interrupt...

157
00:16:13,001 --> 00:16:17,000
...which really means
that you cannot test your program.

158
00:16:19,001 --> 00:16:26,000
I knew that if we designed the program
with insufficient care...

159
00:16:26,001 --> 00:16:30,000
...we would end up with something...

160
00:16:32,001 --> 00:16:35,000
...which, once entered
into the computer...

161
00:16:38,001 --> 00:16:42,000
...wouldn't work, and would make
irreproducible errors...

162
00:16:42,001 --> 00:16:49,000
...the cause of which we would be
unable to determine.

163
00:16:50,001 --> 00:16:52,000
That was the reason...

164
00:16:52,001 --> 00:16:59,000
...why we applied all possible control
mechanisms in the development stage.

165
00:17:06,001 --> 00:17:09,000
EWD1284: "Elegance is not a dispensable luxury..."

166
00:17:10,001 --> 00:17:14,000
...but a factor that decides
between success and failure.

167
00:17:17,001 --> 00:17:20,000
One of the things I discovered
as early as the 1960's...

168
00:17:20,001 --> 00:17:26,000
...is that mathematical elegance...

169
00:17:27,001 --> 00:17:32,000
...is NOT a question of aesthetics,
nor of taste or fashion...

170
00:17:32,001 --> 00:17:36,000
...but something you can translate
into a technical notion.

171
00:17:37,001 --> 00:17:45,000
The Concise Oxford Dictionary gives
as one of the meanings of 'elegant'...

172
00:17:47,001 --> 00:17:50,000
...'ingeniously simple and effective'.

173
00:17:54,001 --> 00:17:58,000
In practice a program is manageable...

174
00:17:59,001 --> 00:18:04,000
...if you make a truly elegant program...

175
00:18:05,001 --> 00:18:08,000
...firstly because it is shorter
than most alternatives...

176
00:18:09,001 --> 00:18:13,000
...and consists of discrete parts...

177
00:18:14,001 --> 00:18:18,000
...each of which you can replace
by an alternative implementation...

178
00:18:19,001 --> 00:18:23,000
...without influencing
the rest of the program.

179
00:18:26,001 --> 00:18:28,000
But also, curiously...

180
00:18:33,001 --> 00:18:37,000
...the most elegant programs
are often the most efficient.

181
00:18:45,001 --> 00:18:49,000
When there were no computers,
programming was no problem.

182
00:18:49,001 --> 00:18:53,000
When we had a few weak computers,
it became a mild problem.

183
00:18:54,001 --> 00:18:59,080
Now that we have gigantic computers,
programming is a gigantic problem.

184
00:19:02,001 --> 00:19:08,000
My first programming years were
a bit strange compared to now...

185
00:19:08,001 --> 00:19:12,000
...in that I programmed
for non-existing machines.

186
00:19:12,001 --> 00:19:16,000
My friends Bram Loopstra
and Carel Scholten built the machine...

187
00:19:17,001 --> 00:19:21,000
...and in the meantime
I wrote the relevant software.

188
00:19:23,001 --> 00:19:29,000
I was used to not testing a program...

189
00:19:30,001 --> 00:19:33,000
...because the machine to test it on
wasn't finished.

190
00:19:34,001 --> 00:19:37,000
I knew right away
that you had to create something...

191
00:19:38,001 --> 00:19:41,000
...that you could keep
under your intellectual control.

192
00:20:18,001 --> 00:20:23,000
I started to work at the Mathematical
Centre in March 1952.

193
00:20:24,001 --> 00:20:29,000
My wife to be had worked there
since the summer of 1949.

194
00:20:30,001 --> 00:20:33,000
She had a job as a calculator.

195
00:20:34,001 --> 00:20:37,000
I was taken on as a programmer.

196
00:20:40,001 --> 00:20:42,000
And I rather liked her.

197
00:20:43,001 --> 00:20:50,000
Our first date was to the concert at the
occasion of the Mathematical Congress...

198
00:20:51,001 --> 00:20:54,000
...in 1954 in Amsterdam.

199
00:20:54,001 --> 00:20:56,000
Then the news was out...

200
00:20:57,001 --> 00:21:00,000
...that I was sweet on her.

201
00:21:02,001 --> 00:21:05,000
People complemented me on my taste.

202
00:21:19,001 --> 00:21:23,000
Professionally, I was strongly influenced
by my mother.

203
00:21:23,001 --> 00:21:27,000
She was a brilliant mathematician.

204
00:21:27,001 --> 00:21:32,000
I remember when I had bought my books
for the next school year...

205
00:21:32,001 --> 00:21:39,000
...I saw the geometry book, which
scared me with all those Greek letters.

206
00:21:39,001 --> 00:21:43,000
I asked my mother if geometry was hard.

207
00:21:44,001 --> 00:21:45,000
She said: not at all.

208
00:21:46,001 --> 00:21:49,000
Make sure you know all the formulae
by heart...

209
00:21:50,001 --> 00:21:55,000
...and if you need more than five lines,
you're on the wrong track.

210
00:22:00,001 --> 00:22:05,000
Hard toy...
'Bikkel'. No fun in that.

211
00:22:08,001 --> 00:22:12,000
(Dutch) 's Laands wijs, 's lands err... (Dutch)
Coult it be 'volkslied'?

212
00:22:12,001 --> 00:22:14,000
Oh, 'wijs'. I see.

213
00:22:18,001 --> 00:22:21,000
Does that fit with anything?

214
00:22:22,001 --> 00:22:28,000
The V of 3 down?
Yes, and the i of 'bikkel'.

215
00:22:28,001 --> 00:22:31,000
My rashness is being rewarded.

216
00:22:32,001 --> 00:22:36,000
Why has elegance found
so little following?

217
00:22:36,001 --> 00:22:39,000
That's the reality of it.

218
00:22:41,001 --> 00:22:45,000
Elegance has the disadvantage,
if that's what it is...

219
00:22:49,001 --> 00:22:52,000
...that hard work is needed
to achieve it...

220
00:22:53,001 --> 00:22:56,000
...and a good education
to appreciate it.